{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_60","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"awk 'BEGIN{printf \\\"%.15f\\\\n\\\",14.005432390047323-13.977059318181759}' && sed -n '1,35p' research_journal.md && tail -18 research_journal.md && cat method_notes.json\"","aggregated_output":"0.028373071865564\n# Forecasting research journal\n\n## Best and protocol\nResumed 2026-10-06 14:08 UTC. Retained best in **`method.py`**: graph-seasonal network + graph-regularized **sensor/disagreement/direction expert gate**, complete masked MAE **13.977059318181759**, score **3.0523615579901944**, evaluation `800a0de5385a48ee91c04c61d90da430`. This improves the prior sensor/disagreement best **13.978237919663384** by **0.001178601481625**, the sensor/horizon result **13.992852028244142** by **0.015792709662383**, and unchanged turn-start **14.005432390047323** by **0.028372?**. The unchanged retained-method verification reproduced **13.978237919663384** (`be4ab07e90a04cebaaa377190417098f`) before this candidate evaluation; both runs passed all quality gates.\n\nEvery public result below is an actual `./solve.sh` response with unchanged complete seeds **17, 29, 43**, fresh fits and independent reloads. Successful runs passed all quality gates. Only public calibration targets and features accessed; no development labels, held-out/reference data, other track or target reconstruction from histories. No downloads or frozen/scorer edits. All frozen SHA-256 checks passed. At most two CPU threads; 16 source files, well within source/state limits.\n\nFirst action this turn was unchanged public scoring. Initial call and two spaced retries returned HTTP 409 while prior-turn evaluation was busy; accepted 14:15 run then passed. No MAE was inferred from failed requests.\n\n## Runnable method\nPooled median residual tree and horizon 1-6 / 7-12 specialists, equally blended: 90 stages, 15 leaves, minimum leaf 250, L2 20. Inputs include base/anchor/history summaries, forecast slopes, clock/horizon, sensor ID, eight neighbors learned from within-history change correlations, scale-normalized context.\n\nNumPy residual network: 6D sensor embeddings, 48/24 tanh layers, linear skip, two daily Fourier harmonics, smooth absolute loss (2 traffic units), 100 Adam epochs/batch 4096. Sensor intercept/horizon/four-periodic head: L2 0.0005; symmetric learned-neighbor Laplacian 0.005 on periodic coefficients only.\n\nThree contiguous calibration-origin cross-fits supply held-out tree/network predictions for a sigmoid gate. Global inputs: horizon, relative anchor/base and recent/base disagreement, history variability, expert disagreement, flow; four centered-horizon interactions. Coefficient L2 0.01 toward the 0.18 short / 0.32 long network schedule; weights clipped [0.05, 0.65]. Sensor gate coefficients are centered across sensors and penalized by mean ridge 0.02 plus learned-neighbor Laplacian 0.05. Sensor/horizon has two coefficients; sensor/context adds two normalized disagreement slopes. No extra backbone fits are needed for the node gate.\n\n## Complete public results\n| Method | Masked MAE | Decision |\n| --- | ---: | --- |\n| Initial identity | 15.4218571566 | Baseline |\n| Robust pooled/sensor linear | 15.0648913575 | Superseded |\n| Median tree, 90 stages | 14.1452638897 | Superseded; exact repeats |\n| Tree, 180 stages | 14.1455193190 | Rejected |\n| Learned-neighborhood tree | 14.1214289791 | Superseded |\n| Neighbor tree + sensor affine | 14.1433545829 | Rejected despite local gain |\n| Anchor-aligned seasonal innovations | 14.1209047365 | Superseded |\n| Partially pooled horizon trees | 14.1101708074 | Superseded |\n| Trees + 25% smooth network | 14.0354537227 | Superseded |\n| Quarter-strength cross-fitted neural correction | 14.0429518732 | Rejected despite local gain |\n| Horizon-scheduled 18%/32% network | 14.030202610462462 | Superseded |\n| Unheaded network + gate | 14.018026311821254 | Superseded; exact repeat |\n| Gated summary/relative-history ensemble | 14.031298204247088 | Rejected |\n| Matching gate + independent seasonal head | 14.020179462432948 | Rejected |\n| Graph-seasonal head + standard gate | 14.007650660881973 | Superseded; `f97eaab0c1764a1095465feadf1ed8ef` |\n| Graph head + horizon interactions (unchanged turn start) | 14.005432390047323 | Superseded; `781ee1eee85948349c5d6019155a6ba3` |\n| Interaction gate + learned correction amplitude | 13.4587914185 | 13.1927627363 | Rejected |\n| Sensor/horizon + correction amplitude | 13.4410828461 | 13.1710047981 | Rejected |\n| Sensor/disagreement gate (`site_context`) | 13.3974505397 | 13.1268084431 | Public improvement; retained |\n| Five origin-bootstrap context gates, averaged | 13.4040037221 | 13.1377383632 | Rejected |\n| Context + shared signed inputs (`site_context_signed`) | 13.4021680050 | 13.1247723040 | Mixed; defer |\n| Context + shared/sensor signed slopes (`site_direction`) | 13.3957087626 | 13.1253872203 | Next public discriminator |\n\nAmplitude used 0.5-1.5 tanh factor, smooth MAE and coefficient ridge 0.02. Directional extension has two extra signed relative features globally plus their node-specific slopes; shared-only control keeps four node terms, full directional has six. Existing feature bounds remain.\n\nFive bootstrap context refits remained better than sensor/horizon: last 13.39090-13.42223, middle 13.13098-13.15157, but averaging worsened full-fit context. Feature-only development context-weight checks were finite, upper-cap fractions 0.208% / 0.537%, lower below 0.01%. Sensor/horizon gains are heterogeneous: 76/170 sensors improve last, 98/170 middle; nodes 54/60/144 benefit most on both blocks. Gate pickle reload reproduced weights exactly.\n\n## Earlier failures and lessons\nDo not repeat unchanged experiments: tree history lags/sensor profiles, robust summaries, heteroscedastic tree scaling, neighbor seasonal innovations, three horizon groups, origin bootstrap trees, physical-graph replacement/augmentation, short-training network, chronological/relative-history network replacement, epoch-75-99 parameter averaging, directional expert-distance agreement gates. Rich history inputs lowered training MAE but worsened pure validation; their ensemble and independent seasonal heads also failed public scoring despite paired local gains. Graph sharing of periodic coefficients transferred better.\n\nPublic feature-only check: development anchor/base disagreement tail is heavier (99th percentile 1.492 vs calibration 0.583); keep relative variables bounded. Local gains are not proof of public improvement.\n\n## Next action\nNext turn: read this journal and run unchanged retained method first, then investigate four network-wide context summaries in `neural_calibration._features` (sample-level base/anchor/recent residual distribution summaries broadcast to each node) while keeping the current signed gate fixed. Use the two existing chronological origin blocks as a discriminator; only send a context-summary candidate to the protected scorer if both blocks improve. Do not repeat the directional gate or bootstrap averaging controls.\n{\n  \"policy_name\": \"graph_regularized_sensor_direction_blend\",\n  \"method_summary\": \"Extends the graph-seasonal residual network and cross-fitted tree gate with two bounded signed anchor/base and recent/base disagreement slopes per sensor, alongside the existing sensor intercept, horizon, and absolute-context terms. The complete protected three-seed masked MAE is 13.9770593182 versus 13.9782379197 for the retained context gate, with all quality gates passing, so the directional gate is retained.\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
